 "gate1_verdict": "On H1 the two are TIED WITHIN NOISE, not separated: 5/14 for the baseline against 4/14 for the CA is a ONE-MODEL margin, and both sit far above the ~0.88/14 expected by chance while far below a capability. The prereg's rule counts this as the baseline matching; the honest reading is that neither battery attributes families from four numbers. K1 DOES NOT FIRE: the baseline loses on best pair separation 0.5x vs the CA's 2.4x. The CA-derived features carry something the static two-token conditional does not, so H3 survives its first real test and Gate 2 is licensed. This does NOT establish that the dynamics are necessary -- only that the cheapest deflation tried so far does not reproduce them. COHERENCE, CORRECTED: 3 baseline feature(s) clear an uncorrected p<0.05 (fixed_point_fraction, cond_top1_mean, cond_entropy_mean), but after Benjamini-Hochberg over the four tests actually run, NONE SURVIVES -- and fixed_point_fraction sit within Monte-Carlo error of the 0.05 boundary, so they are NOT DECIDABLE at 100,000 permutations rather than negative. Gate 0's CA attractor share was 0.218 at p~3e-4, which survives any correction over its three tests. So the CA feature coheres within families more tightly than any static-conditional feature does, and the difference is not marginal. Exploratory-to-registered boundary: the COMPARISON is pre-registered in prereg.json and K1's rule was frozen before any of these numbers existed; the numbers themselves are new. Family is the independent unit throughout, so the six Pythia sizes are one observation.",
